From 7f18f5acbe40fbf0a06296882a66f72e3126f683 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 21:19:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08105/11362] The budget the census pinned was the scored one
 for one game in five

`make_carnot_agent` passes no explore_budget, so E3AgentPolicy resolves it
per-game through `_route_explore_budget`: 24 for a program_editor route, 80 for
graph_explore. The action-provenance census pinned 24 everywhere. Resolved live,
that is the scored value for tn36 alone -- ft09, lp85, tr87 and vc33 all route to
80 and ran at a third of it.

It is not a cosmetic knob. It is the stall threshold that decides when the induce
path fires, and via `_active_transitions()` it is also how much of the agent's own
evidence the induction prompt is built from. So "the induced world model was not
accurate enough" and "the induced world model saw 25 transitions where the scored
agent would have supplied 81" are different findings, and that run cannot separate
them.

Direction, split rather than waved at: the headline plan-derived share SURVIVES,
because an earlier stall fires the pipeline sooner and leaves more of the 400
actions for it -- 3.6% pooled is an upper bound on the scored config, not an
under-count. The run's own data agrees, the one game measured at its correct budget
being the one with by far the highest share (15.4% vs <=1.3%). The per-game
"where it is lost" verdicts do NOT survive unqualified, so each now carries an
explore_budget_scope with one of three verdicts. vc33 gets the third: every
induction it ran was a level_up_reinduction, which `_should_enter_induction`
prioritises over the stall and which feeds on the post-boundary transition slice
(count 1 in every observed event), so its induce-evidence channel is not confounded
even though its budget was mis-set. Retracting it alongside the stall-triggered
games would have been the easy wrong answer.

Forward: --explore-budget now defaults to "routed" in the worker and driver, and
each cell records the resolved integer plus how it was chosen, so a future census
reproduces the scored configuration instead of repeating this.

Two things the regeneration itself exposed. `supersedes_artifact_raw` had been
typed straight into artifact.json rather than emitted by the builder, so the first
re-derivation deleted it -- a never-prune violation executed by a script, caught
only by diffing the rebuild against the committed file; it now lives in the
generator. And `git_head` was stamped from the current HEAD on every rebuild, which
would silently re-date live GPU episodes to a commit that did not exist when they
ran; the census commit is now passed explicitly and the report's own commit is
recorded separately.

No measured value moved: the reproducibility checksum is byte-identical, nothing
was removed, and the only changed fields are run_date and the two prose fields that
were wrong.

